Movie audio: a new movie starts without the audio the movie before left pending. God of War reuses one sceMpeg for
every movie. At the end of a movie its audio callback (0x18A8F8) refuses packets while its ring is full, and the demux
stub keeps them to deliver first on the next demux call. The next movie's open (0x189BD0) resets the ring and the
0x28-byte header count, then the stale packets went in: their first 40 bytes became the "header", the new movie's real
SShd/SSbd header was stored as samples, and every 512-byte L/R block pair was shifted. PT-BR A01MP02 after the intro:
7 packets, 26,768 + 40 bytes = 184 bytes into each pair, the left output taking the right channel for 92 of every 256
samples: a 187.5 Hz buzz, heard as crackling/blown voices in the cutscenes (the USA disc's packing left a whole number
of pairs by chance). The fix: when the game registers its stream callbacks and no input of the movie was demuxed yet,
pending audio is dropped (libmpeg keeps no input between demux calls); a state that already served pictures is reset.
GOW_MPEG_DIAG also logs the state at each registration ([MPEG:diag] AddStrCallback state ...).
Checked with tools/test/movie-smoke.ps1 (A01MP02, warp ATHN01D) and tools/test/movie-audio-compare.py on the raw mix:
PT-BR gain 0.7415 / residual 2245 / 16,813 samples left=source right -> gain 0.9997 / residual 1.18 / 0;
USA unchanged (0.9998 / 1.17 / 0). A player session (PT-BR, a02mp02 and mewintro after other movies): 6 and 5 stale
packets dropped, the movie track mixed at gain 1.00, the crackling gone. Remaining, separate: one 4,096-frame ring
repeat per PT-BR movie (refill timing), and the level music some movies leave playing by design.

diff --git a/PS2Recomp/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp b/PS2Recomp/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp
--- a/PS2Recomp/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp
+++ b/PS2Recomp/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp
@@ -2170,7 +2170,30 @@
             std::fprintf(stderr, "[MPEG:diag] AddStrCallback type=%u ch=%u func=%08x data=%08x\n", streamType, streamId, callbackFunc, callbackData);
         std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(g_mpeg_stub_mutex);
         g_mpeg_stub_state.initialized = true;
-        (void)getPlaybackState(mpegAddr);
+        if (std::getenv("GOW_MPEG_DIAG"))
+        {
+            const MpegPlaybackState &state = getPlaybackState(mpegAddr);
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "[MPEG:diag] AddStrCallback state pictures=%u sawInput=%d ended=%d pss=%zu pendingAudio=%zu callbacks=%zu\n",
+                         state.picturesServed, state.sawInput ? 1 : 0, state.streamEnded ? 1 : 0, state.pssBuffer.size(),
+                         g_pendingAudio.count(mpegAddr) ? g_pendingAudio[mpegAddr].size() : size_t{0},
+                         g_mpeg_stub_state.callbacksByMpeg.count(mpegAddr) ? g_mpeg_stub_state.callbacksByMpeg[mpegAddr].size() : size_t{0});
+        }
+        // gow-native-pc (patch 54): God of War reuses one sceMpeg for every movie and registers its stream callbacks
+        // before the first demux of each one, after its movie open reset the audio ring and header count (0x189BD0).
+        // Audio packets its callback refused at the end of the movie before (ring full) still waited here and went
+        // first into the new ring: the new movie's 0x28-byte header was then stored as samples and every 512-byte
+        // L/R block pair was shifted (PT-BR A01MP02 after the intro: 7 packets, 26,768 + 40 bytes, 184 bytes into
+        // each pair: a 187.5 Hz buzz with the channels swapped). No input of this movie was demuxed yet, so anything
+        // pending belongs to the one before; libmpeg keeps no input between demux calls. A state that already served
+        // pictures is the movie before as well.
+        MpegPlaybackState &state = getPlaybackState(mpegAddr);
+        if (state.picturesServed != 0u)
+        {
+            state = makeFreshPlaybackState();
+            g_mpeg_stub_state.callbacksByMpeg.erase(mpegAddr);
+        }
+        if (!state.sawInput)
+            g_pendingAudio.erase(mpegAddr);
         const uint32_t handle = g_mpeg_stub_state.nextCallbackHandle++;
         g_mpeg_stub_state.callbacksByMpeg[mpegAddr].push_back(
             MpegRegisteredCallback{streamType, streamId, callbackFunc, callbackData, handle, true});
